{
  "dom_id": "step-q125863",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226336/assets/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?",
  "worked_solution": "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n{{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{⏟}}_{1\\text{st}},\\,{{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,{{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,{{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{th}}\\,{{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{th}},\\,{{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{⏟}}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$",
